062408447265625, -0.01175689697265625, -0.004016876220703125, 0.025115966796875, 0.00925445556640625, -0.0009212493896484375, 0.035369873046875, -0.015960693359375, -0.0157318115234375, -0.0196685791015625, -0.0024623870849609375, 0.0264739990234375, 0.048858642578125, -0.01084136962890625, 0.00360870361328125, -0.0222625732421875, -0.01137542724609375, 0.0285186767578125, -0.0166168212890625, 0.02001953125, -0.004550933837890625, -0.034332275390625, -0.0052032470703125, 0.019195556640625, -0.0135040283203125, 0.004146575927734375, 0.035919189453125, -0.00870513916015625, -0.00836181640625, 0.0011663436889648438, 0.004070281982421875, -0.0102691650390625, -6.985664367675781e-05, 0.01523590087890625, -0.0093841552734375, -0.016021728515625, 0.00865936279296875, -0.0239410400390625, -0.008941650390625, -0.0014905929565429688, -0.00830841064453125, 0.03887939453125, -0.0247344970703125, -0.0016260147094726562, 0.0165252685546875, 0.01332855224609375, 0.0090179443359375, -0.04327392578125, -0.01605224609375, -0.001682281494140625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) raises important questions about governance and control: Who sets direction, and when? How much autonomy should AI systems receive\u2014and through which mechanisms do humans provide guidance? This article investigates when, how, and through what mechanisms humans guide AI work, and how these patterns change depending on modes of human-AI interaction. The following sections outline core concepts and definitions, explain how these ideas shape the research design, describe methodology, report and interpret findings, and finally assess limitations and boundary conditions.\n\n**Key Concepts and Definitions**\n\nTo ground the reader, we provide a structured glossary of essential terms as used in this study:\n\n- **Node:** A user group or unique organizational unit within the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI) dataset.\n- **Node-month-task:** A unique unit defined by user group (\u201cnode\u201d), calendar month, and specific task coded at O*NET level-0.\n- **O*NET level-0:** The most granular task classification from the O*NET system, typically representing detailed occupational tasks (e.g., \"Draft press release\").\n- **Delegation:** Assigning cognitive steps from human to AI.\n- **Prior human direction:** Human intent or specification shaping the task before AI execution.\n- **Delegation score:** The normalized frequency (mean=0, SD=1) of a given delegation mode within a defined unit of analysis, allowing comparison across modes.\n- **Work orientation:** The proportion of AI tasks by a user flagged as work/professional (0 to 1 scale).\n\nWe distinguish between two principal modes of delegation, each defined by the timing and extent of human input. Table 1 summarizes definitions, boundary rules, and illustrative examples, including concrete gray-area cases. Brief footnotes are used for additional technical terms, as needed. (Supplementary Figure 1 in the data repository provides a decision tree; Supplementary Table S1 shows a sectoral sample breakdown.)\n\n| Mode                 | Core Definition                                                         | Boundary Rule                                                                              | Example(s)                                                                                               |\n|----------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Specified delegation | Human sets explicit goals or constraints up front; minimal feedback after initial instructions, before output is finalized. | If substantial feedback or adjustment occurs after generation begins, not considered purely specified. All prompt engineering before initial content generation is specified. | User submits a detailed prompt (\u201cSummarize X in 200 words, covering points A, B, C\u201d) and accepts the first summary without changes. Tuning or optimizing prompts before clicking 'submit' (even through trial and error) also counts as specified delegation. Editing the prompt multiple times before hitting 'submit' is still specified delegation if the AI has not produced output. **Gray case example:** A user repeatedly rewrites a prompt for greater clarity or tone, but does not accept any AI-generated output until ready; this remains specified delegation. |\n| Iterative coproduction | Human collaborates interactively, providing feedback or adjustments after initial AI output. | If user reviews and revises outputs interactively after creation has begun (i.e., after any AI content is generated), it is iterative coproduction regardless of initial instructions. | User submits a prompt, receives an AI-generated draft, then asks for specific changes (\u201cExpand section 2,\u201d \u201cMake the tone more formal\u201d) and repeats this loop. **Gray case example:** A user requests an AI-generated text, accepts most of the first output, but highlights a single paragraph for immediate revision; since intervention occurs post-generation, this counts as iterative coproduction. Another gray case: The user clicks \"regenerate\" after reviewing the output to see alternatives\u2014every subsequent review-and-regenerate cycle is iterative coproduction. |\n\n**Resolving Gray Cases:** Any modification, experimentation, or refinement *before* the AI produces output\u2014no matter how many prompt edits\u2014counts as specified delegation. All feedback, correction, or direction occurring *after* AI output generation falls under iterative coproduction.\n\n**Connecting Concepts to Research Design**\n\nThe delegation typology is central to the research questions and empirical strategy. Understanding whether users direct AI systems upfront or continuously through feedback informs how governance and responsibility are distributed. These modes structure the forthcoming analysis: quantifying the prevalence of each across interfaces and exploring what drives their adoption.\n\n**Research Questions**\n\nTo guide the study, we consider:\n1. At which stages are humans most influential in shaping AI output during task delegation?\n2. How do delegation patterns differ between structured (API-based) and interactive (chat-based) environments?\n\n**Methodology**\n\nThis section details the data, variables, modeling approach, and operationalization, with transitional signposting for clarity.\n\n1. **Sample and Data Preprocessing**  \n   The analysis uses the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI), comprising 2.1 million anonymized human-AI interactions (April\u2013May 2026), each with metadata on task, user group (\u201cnode\u201d), and process. The sample is about 80% U.S.-based, English-speaking office professionals (administration, technology, communications, business); creative, manufacturing, and non-English tasks are underrepresented (see Supplementary Table S1). Records are standardized to O*NET level-0 task codes for maximum granularity. Analyses are conducted at the node-month-task level, allowing fine-grained assessment of task delegation patterns and control for temporal or organizational cycles.\n\n2. **Operationalization of \"Work Orientation\"**  \n   Work orientation measures the share of each node's AI tasks marked as professional (range: 0 to 1). For each node-month, we model a counterfactual 10 percentage point increase in work orientation (e.g., from 0.40 to 0.50). This increment represents a realistic, moderate change: roughly the observed interquartile range across node-months, and plausible for teams whose AI use gradually shifts toward professional tasks over a year. Simulating such an increase, rather than waiting for rare full real-world transitions, allows causal inference within the observational dataset and helps estimate the effect of increased professionalization on delegation patterns. Still, as a synthetic intervention, the results indicate modeled associations\u2014potential, not guaranteed, behavioral shifts.\n\n3. **Modeling Approach and Statistical Analysis**  \n   Linear regressions estimate the association between a modeled 10% increase in work orientation and prevalence of each delegation mode (normalized delegation score, mean=0, SD=1 per node-month-task). Node and month fixed effects account for unmeasured time or group effects, and covariates for task type (O*NET level-0), user sector, and usage volume control for observable heterogeneity. Analyses are restricted to node-months with both API and Claude.ai (chat) records (n=1,411), supporting within-group comparisons and minimizing interface self-selection bias. Sensitivity checks include alternative model specifications (logit, inclusion of task complexity, and stratification by user AI experience). Results were robust to these variations: effect directions and significance were unchanged (see below and Supplementary Table S2). Statistical reporting uses robust standard errors clustered by node. Full regression and robustness results are summarized in Appendix Table A1.\n\n*Table A1: Key Regression Model Parameters (see Appendix for full details)*  \n| Variable                | Coefficient (API) | Coefficient (Claude.ai) | 95% CI              | Covariates        |\n|-------------------------|-------------------|-------------------------|---------------------|-------------------|\n| Work orientation (+10%) | 2.76 (specified), -0.30 (iterative) | 1.45 (specified), +0.15 (iterative) | [2.30, 3.22], etc. | Task type, sector, usage volume, fixed effects |\n\n**Findings and Interpretation**\n\nThis section summarizes empirical results, explaining their significance for both theory and practical application.\n\n_Empirical Results:_\n- A simulated 10 percentage point increase in work orientation (e.g., raising professional use from 40% to 50% of tasks in a node-month) leads to **specified delegation** rising by 2.76 standard deviations in API environments (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) and by 1.45 in Claude.ai (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]). To contextualize: this shift corresponds to an estimated increase in the absolute share of \u201chighly specified up-front instructions\u201d from a median ~40% of tasks to about 85%\u2014a practical doubling of such instances.\n- **Iterative coproduction** decreases in API mode by 0.30 SD but increases slightly in Claude.ai (chat) by 0.15 SD. The between-interface difference (0.45 SD) is statistically significant.\n- Subgroup analyses reveal that administration and business users show greater growth in specified delegation, while creative and communication sectors experience modestly increased iterative coproduction via chat. Gray cases, especially for ambiguous or complex tasks, are more likely resolved through iterative coproduction\u2014regardless of interface\u2014for less-experienced users.\n- **Robustness:** Sensitivity and robustness checks (i.e., using alternative regression forms, including/excluding covariates, stratifying by AI experience or task complexity) produced consistent findings: effect sizes varied slightly but did not alter the overall conclusions.\n\n_Interpretation and Implications:_\n- In professionalized, work-oriented contexts, APIs propel users toward highly structured, up-front task specification\u2014a \u201cset and execute\u201d approach with little feedback or iteration. Chat modes (e.g., Claude.ai), meanwhile, support more interactive collaboration and real-time oversight, especially for open-ended or creative tasks.\n- The practical implication is clear: if organizations or teams expect extensive iterative collaboration, chat interfaces better facilitate that model, while API integrations encourage streamlined, pre-specified workflows. Mismatches between interface and user expectations could hinder task outcomes or user satisfaction.\n\n**Limitations and Boundary Conditions**\n\nTo provide a clear sense of this study\u2019s scope and caveats, we note the following:\n\n- **Sample Representat"}}